WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.180 --> 00:00:06.060
Film Courage: Do either of you have any good
shark stories that happened before the filming

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:07.060
or after?

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:13.140
Matthew Modine: I don’t have any shark stories,
I just have a tremendous respect for sharks.

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:20.390
They’re prehistoric animals, they are beautiful,
they have a tremendous importance to the sea,

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:25.230
the life of the sea…what’s the word I
want?Yani Gellman: The ecosystem?

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:30.240
Matthew Modine: The ecosystem of the ocean
and I know that they are quickly becoming

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:34.530
extinct because we are hurting about a hundred
million of them a year for their fins, for

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:38.149
soup and for sport.

00:00:38.149 --> 00:00:42.720
And so when we think of the predator of the
sea, really the biggest predator of the sea

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:43.720
is man.

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:46.300
Yani Gellman: And what’s interesting about
this film is that the sharks are not being

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:48.730
hunted, it’s the people who are hunted.

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:52.820
I feel that there is a kind of poetic justice
to the sea.

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:58.990
The sharks and the environment will bite back
if we don’t treat it with proper respect.

00:00:58.990 --> 00:01:04.409
Film Courage: There is a scene in the film
where the two sisters are 47 meters down and

00:01:04.409 --> 00:01:11.370
are in a precarious situation and one confesses
to the other that they felt competitive [in

00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:12.370
life as sisters].

00:01:12.370 --> 00:01:16.540
Has anyone ever confessed to you that they
were competitive with you in regard to an

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:17.540
acting career?

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:18.540
And I’m wondering how you took that?

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:19.540
Because it could be a compliment.

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:20.540
But it could also make you competitive.

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:21.690
Matthew Modine: That’s a good question.

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:23.070
Yani Gellman: That’s a really good question.

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:24.350
Matthew Modine: That’s a very good question.

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:30.659
I know that the most successful actors I’ve
worked with (I won’t have to name any names)

00:01:30.659 --> 00:01:35.980
that they can be very friendly and cooperative,
and then when they put the clapper and say

00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:43.290
action, they would step on your throat to
beat you in a scene.

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:48.741
And it was so funny to me because the education
that I received when I was studying to be

00:01:48.741 --> 00:01:53.210
an actor is that you play with one another,
that you are scene partners.

00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:57.680
That if you’re a good tennis player and
the person I’m playing against is not very

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:02.780
good, what I have to do is improve their game
because I’m not going to be able to have

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:07.700
a good game and show you what kind of a player
I am if the other player can’t hit the ball

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:08.700
back to me.

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:15.109
If I’m just smashing the ball and making
that person run all over the place, it’s

00:02:15.109 --> 00:02:16.459
not going to work.

00:02:16.459 --> 00:02:19.290
You want to try to make the scene as best
as possible.

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:21.650
Yani Gellman: I’ll name a name.

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:25.129
When we got to set everyone is nervous and
excited.

00:02:25.129 --> 00:02:29.439
And literally the moment I met Matthew he
just put us (and you don’t know what to

00:02:29.439 --> 00:02:30.569
expect), he just put us all at ease.

00:02:30.569 --> 00:02:36.010
And we talked about this, he had this way
of making us feel like we were being looked

00:02:36.010 --> 00:02:42.099
out for and being cared for and that when
the camera was on us he would be there for

00:02:42.099 --> 00:02:45.439
us making sure that we got our best performances
out.

00:02:45.439 --> 00:02:48.730
But that also when we’re going out into
the ocean, we’re doing these scuba diving

00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:52.730
things and such, someone is actually watching
to make sure things are being done safely.

00:02:52.730 --> 00:03:00.299
And Matthew just has so much experience and
we all just remarked at how much is you, but

00:03:00.299 --> 00:03:04.340
is actually the character as being the captain
in the film, he took the responsibility that

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:09.079
we were looked out for and I owe you a tremendous
thanks for that because you made our work

00:03:09.079 --> 00:03:10.079
better.

00:03:10.079 --> 00:03:11.079
You really did.

00:03:11.079 --> 00:03:12.079
Matthew Modine: Thank you.

00:03:12.079 --> 00:03:15.189
Film Courage: How is a role like this offered
to either one of you?

00:03:15.189 --> 00:03:18.079
Do you have to audition or do you just get
a call one day?

00:03:18.079 --> 00:03:21.540
Yani Gellman: Well, I have to audition…I
don’t think Matthew has to audition anymore,

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:27.669
especially after all those great films you’ve
done but I did a series of auditions.

00:03:27.669 --> 00:03:31.840
The final audition was actually a Skype session
with the director Johannes and I happened

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:34.089
to be in Yosemite at the time with my Mom.

00:03:34.089 --> 00:03:37.969
And it was a trip we’d been planning for
months so I couldn’t bail on it.

00:03:37.969 --> 00:03:40.579
So they said we will Skype you in from Yosemite.

00:03:40.579 --> 00:03:47.309
So I was there with the mountains in the background
just reading these lines and doing scuba stuff

00:03:47.309 --> 00:03:52.689
and it was the funniest audition I’d ever
had and it somehow clicked and worked and

00:03:52.689 --> 00:03:54.309
the next thing I know I was in the Dominican
Republic.

00:03:54.309 --> 00:03:58.389
Matthew Modine: I remember you talking about
it.

00:03:58.389 --> 00:04:06.059
They sent [me] the script and it’s always
flattering for someone to think of you for

00:04:06.059 --> 00:04:10.169
their film, and send you the script and ask
you to participate.

00:04:10.169 --> 00:04:15.510
And the reason I wanted to do this one was
because my father was a drive-in theater manager

00:04:15.510 --> 00:04:21.019
and these were the kind of films that my dad
really enjoyed playing at the movies because

00:04:21.019 --> 00:04:26.770
it was such an audience-pleaser, that they
loved this kind of movie, like a great summer

00:04:26.770 --> 00:04:34.539
movie that is filled with all of the elements
of a horror film but without the supernatural

00:04:34.539 --> 00:04:36.460
aspects to it.

00:04:36.460 --> 00:04:42.909
I wish my dad were alive to see it because
I know he’d really love the movie.

00:04:42.909 --> 00:04:45.330
Film Courage: Great.

00:04:45.330 --> 00:04:46.330
Thank you!

00:04:46.330 --> 00:04:46.830
Thanks guys!

